-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b93e6386-2a65-58ef-aefe-2334fd66785c', 'rg11-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Network Underfoot$q$, $q$Beneath a forest floor lies a structure that most hikers never consider. Threadlike fungi wrap around the roots of many trees and spread outward through the soil, forming networks that can link one tree to its neighbors. The relationship between tree and fungus is reciprocal: each partner gives something and receives something in return. The tree supplies sugars made through photosynthesis, and the fungus delivers water and minerals it has gathered from soil the roots cannot reach.

Scientists have studied these partnerships for well over a century, but research into how far the networks extend is still nascent. Only in recent decades have tools existed that can trace a single substance as it moves from one tree, through the fungus, into another. Early experiments suggested that carbon could travel between trees this way, a finding that drew wide attention.

Some popular accounts quickly described the networks as a kind of forest internet, through which older trees deliberately nourish their young. Many researchers are more circumspect. They point out that a substance moving between trees does not prove that any tree intended to send it, and that the fungus may simply be serving its own needs. They also note that results from one forest may not hold in another with different soil, species, or climate.

This caution is not a rejection of the research. It reflects the ordinary way science advances, by testing a striking claim until its limits become clear. Some early studies have been repeated with similar results; others have not. The picture that is emerging is more complicated than the popular version, though no less remarkable.

For forest managers, even the cautious version matters. If fungal networks help seedlings survive, then clearing land in ways that tear up the soil could damage more than the trees that are removed. Protecting the ground beneath the forest may turn out to be as important as protecting the trees above it.$q$, 'draft')
