-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('81c33a08-8435-5273-a480-ca2312ce12f4', 'rg11-context-clues-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When Every Town Kept Its Own Time$q$, $q$Before the late 1800s, most towns kept their own time. Each community set its clocks by the sun, declaring noon at the moment the sun stood highest overhead. Because the sun reaches that point at slightly different moments as one moves east or west, a town a hundred kilometers away might run a few minutes ahead of or behind its neighbor. For centuries, these idiosyncratic local times caused little trouble, since few people traveled far enough or fast enough to notice.

Railroads changed that. A train could pass through dozens of towns in a single day, and each station might display a different time. Timetables became bewildering documents, crowded with columns of conversions, and passengers routinely missed connections. Worse, two trains running on the same track according to different clocks could end up in the same place at the same moment. As rail networks grew, the patchwork of local times became untenable; it simply could not continue without risking travelers' safety.

The solution that railway planners conceived was pragmatic rather than elegant. Instead of insisting that every place follow the sun exactly, they divided large regions into broad zones, each one sharing a single standard time. Within a zone, noon on the clock might arrive a little before or after the sun's true noon, but every station agreed. The plan accepted a small inaccuracy in exchange for a large gain in order.

Not everyone welcomed the change. Some residents resented setting their clocks by a railroad's decision, and a few towns kept their old local time for years. Gradually, however, the convenience of a shared schedule won out, and governments eventually adopted the zones into law.

Today, most people never wonder why their clocks agree with those in a city hundreds of kilometers away. That agreement, which now seems natural, was once a deliberate invention born from a very practical problem.$q$, 'draft')
