  ('bcd77041-87a1-5c23-a6fb-a7e001503dd2', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null, 'easy',
   $q$According to the passage, why are many lichens sensitive to pollutants?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They grow so slowly that they cannot replace the parts damaged by nitrogen and sulfur.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-detail"},{"text":"They grow only on the trunks of street trees, where traffic exhaust collects in the bark.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"They depend on sunlight, and polluted air blocks most of the light they need to make food.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"They lack roots and a protective layer, so they take in water and nutrients directly from rain and air.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They lack roots and a protective layer, so they take in water and nutrients directly from rain and air.$q$, $q$The first paragraph says lichens absorb water and nutrients "straight from rain and air" because they have no roots or protective layer, and "that same openness" makes them sensitive. Slow growth is mentioned later, but only to explain why lichens reflect years of conditions.$q$, 'draft', '23cfc83d-ad00-5c09-844e-bd91e4f2b73b')
