   $q$Which evidence best supports the inference that ice factories had a shipping advantage over Brenford's lake ice?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Health inspectors warned about ponds near farms.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-different-claim"},{"text":"Ice cut in January could be sold as late as August.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-opposing-claim"},{"text":"Lake ice sometimes contained leaves and sediment.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-different-claim"},{"text":"Factories made ice close to the cities that bought it.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Factories made ice close to the cities that bought it.$q$, $q$Brenford's ice traveled by rail as far as three hundred miles, while factories produced ice "close to the cities that bought it," so factory ice needed far less shipping. The detail about leaves and sediment supports a difference in purity, not in shipping.$q$, 'draft', '8800749b-2ae2-5f2d-8c44-13606e370353')
