-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7e56376e-1bed-526d-829c-a1b8af742f4b', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Racing the Clock on the Mound$q$, $q$For years, games in the Tri-Valley Summer League, an amateur baseball league for college players, routinely ran past three hours. Before the 2022 season, the league adopted a pitch timer: pitchers had eighteen seconds to begin their motion with the bases empty and twenty-two seconds with runners on base. A batter who was not ready by the eight-second mark was charged with a strike.

The effect on game length was immediate. The average nine-inning game fell from three hours and four minutes in 2021 to two hours and thirty-one minutes in 2022. Violations were common in the first two weeks, averaging almost three per game, but they dropped to fewer than one per game by midseason. The league's statistician, Renata Okonkwo-Hale, also found that stolen base attempts rose by nearly forty percent. Under a related new rule, pitchers could throw over to a base only twice per batter without penalty, so runners could take bigger leads.

Not every change was easy to explain. Batting averages rose slightly, from .251 to .259, but Okonkwo-Hale cautioned that the league had also switched to a new ball supplier that season, and no one had tested whether the new balls traveled farther. Several pitchers reported that their arms felt more tired late in games, though the league did not track injuries in either season, so the reports could not be compared with earlier years.

Attendance rose by about twelve percent. Team owners credited the shorter games, pointing to surveys in which fans had listed long games as their main complaint. Okonkwo-Hale has suggested a more careful test, since two seasons is a short record and attendance also rose that summer in a neighboring league that had not adopted a timer.$q$, 'draft')
