-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7c63dc9a-96d8-5fa4-9152-30fcf8624162', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Keep Paper at the Bus Stop, for Now$q$, $q$The Port Aldane Transit Authority has announced that, starting next spring, it will stop posting printed timetables at its 1,100 bus stops. Riders will instead be directed to a phone app and a text message service that give real-time arrival estimates. The authority says the change will save about ninety thousand dollars a year in printing and labor. That is a real saving, but I believe the plan is a mistake, at least in its current form.

Start with who rides the bus. According to the authority's own survey from last year, nearly one in five riders over age sixty-five does not own a smartphone. The same survey found that riders on the three routes serving the county hospital are older, on average, than riders anywhere else in the system. These are exactly the passengers who can least afford to stand at a stop without knowing when the next bus will come.

Supporters of the plan point out that the text service works on any phone, including basic models without internet access. That is true, and it is a genuine improvement over a paper schedule that cannot report delays. But the text service requires riders to know the five-digit code assigned to each stop, and the authority plans to print those codes on small stickers. Anyone who has tried to read a faded sticker on a pole at night will see the problem.

I am not arguing that the city should keep things exactly as they are. Real-time arrival information is better than a timetable when it works. My proposal is modest: keep printed timetables at the roughly two hundred busiest stops and at every stop on the hospital routes, and test the sticker system for a year before removing paper anywhere else. The authority has not said how many riders currently use the app, and until it shares that number, the public has no way to judge whether paper timetables are truly obsolete.$q$, 'draft')
