-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c765a03d-0010-5fed-bd58-8ae1acfa8c8d', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Surveyor of the Kesterel Marshes$q$, $q$In the summer of 1871, a provincial land office sent surveyor Imogen Varga to map the Kesterel Marshes, a maze of channels and reed beds at the mouth of the Tavish River. Earlier maps showed the marshes as a single blank area labeled only 'wetland.' The land office wanted to know whether any of the ground was firm enough for farms or a road.

Varga worked from a flat-bottomed boat with two assistants for eleven weeks. Because tall reeds blocked her view, she could not use the long sight lines that surveyors normally relied on. Instead, she measured short distances with a chain, recorded compass bearings at every bend in a channel, and pushed a marked pole into the mud to test its depth. Her finished map showed forty-one named channels and six islands of firm clay, along with notes on where the mud was too deep for a pole to find bottom.

The land office did not adopt her map. A senior official wrote in the margin of her report that the number of channels seemed 'unlikely,' and a shorter survey completed in 1874 by a different team, which spent nine days in the marsh, showed only nineteen channels. For the next seventy years, most published maps of the region followed the 1874 version.

In 1952, aerial photographs of the marshes were taken for a flood control project. When engineers compared them with old maps, they found that Varga's channels matched the photographs closely, often within a few meters, while the 1874 map left out more than half of them. Historians have not found any record explaining why the land office preferred the later survey. Some have suggested that officials trusted a team of senior surveyors over a younger one, while others note that the 1874 map was cheaper to copy and print because it showed less detail. Varga's original map now hangs in the provincial archive, where it is still consulted by researchers who study how the marsh has changed.$q$, 'draft')
