-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b5771e20-4539-5867-a8a1-125a9250a969', 'rg11-figurative-language-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Tuning Note$q$, $q$Five minutes before the winter concert, the backstage hallway at Larkspur High sounded like a hundred radios arguing at once. Violins squeaked, a tuba let out a low groan, and someone in the percussion section tested a cymbal that made everyone flinch.

Nadia Okafor stood in the middle of it all holding her oboe. It was her job to give the tuning note, the single A that the entire orchestra would match before the first piece. She had played that note in rehearsal a thousand times, yet tonight her hands felt as if they belonged to a stranger.

Her section leader, a senior named Mateo Vargas, leaned against the wall. "The hardest note in the program," he said, "is the one that is supposed to be the easiest."

Nadia frowned. "That makes no sense."

"It will," he said. "Everyone hears it, and nobody is playing anything else to cover it."

When the stage manager waved them on, the hall was dim and quiet. Nadia lifted her reed, took a breath, and played. The A came out thin at first, then steadied, and she felt the strings settle around it like birds landing on a wire. The sound was not loud, but it was firm, and for a moment the whole room seemed to lean toward it.

Afterward, while the audience was still clapping for the final piece, Mateo bumped her shoulder. "See? You were alone up there with sixty people."

She laughed, because now she understood him. The tuning note belonged to her alone, but it only mattered because everyone else was waiting to join it.$q$, 'draft')
