values
  ('90629d86-c200-5435-8b7b-893e8c6a2b1b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The narrator says the sound "was not loud, but it was firm." Why is "firm" a better fit here than "harsh," a word that also describes a strong sound?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Firm\" suggests a very loud sound, while \"harsh\" suggests a sound that is too quiet.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"\"Firm\" suggests the oboe itself was stiff, while \"harsh\" describes the hall's acoustics.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"\"Firm\" suggests steady confidence, while \"harsh\" suggests an unpleasant, grating tone.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Firm\" and \"harsh\" mean the same thing, so the author's choice makes no difference.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Firm" suggests steady confidence, while "harsh" suggests an unpleasant, grating tone.$q$, $q$The room "seemed to lean toward" the note, so the author wants a positive word suggesting steadiness; "harsh" would imply a sound people want to escape. The passage says the sound "was not loud," so "firm" cannot mean loud.$q$, 'draft', 'b5771e20-4539-5867-a8a1-125a9250a969')
