-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cdce2745-3cc2-59f5-9849-e7b32611a803', 'rg11-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Cities Run Hot$q$, $q$On a July afternoon, a downtown parking lot can feel like the inside of an oven, while a park only a few blocks away stays noticeably cooler. Scientists call this pattern the urban heat island effect, and it is one of the clearest examples of how building materials can change the climate of an entire neighborhood.

The cause is not mysterious. Dark asphalt and roofing absorb sunlight during the day and release that stored heat slowly after sunset. Buildings packed closely together trap warm air, and there are fewer trees to provide shade or to cool the air as water evaporates from their leaves. As a result, some city blocks can stay several degrees warmer than nearby suburbs long into the night.

Planners have learned that cooling a city is a matter of small choices repeated thousands of times. A single white roof will not change the temperature of a whole district, but thousands of reflective roofs, combined with new street trees and lighter pavement, can make a measurable difference. In one approach, crews coat older roofs with a pale material that reflects sunlight instead of soaking it up.

There is a paradox at the heart of this work: the most effective cooling tools are often the least dramatic. A row of maples does not look like advanced technology, yet over time it may lower summer temperatures more than an expensive new machine could.

Researchers stress that the benefits are not shared evenly. Neighborhoods with fewer trees often face the hottest summers, so deciding where to plant first is not only a scientific question but also a question of fairness.$q$, 'draft')
