-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('785f8d37-79b3-5bda-9bc0-56a4f5a84eee', 'rg11-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Edges of the Known World$q$, $q$For centuries, mapmakers faced an awkward problem: what should they draw where their knowledge ran out? A coastline might be carefully traced for hundreds of miles and then simply stop, because no traveler had reported what came next. Some mapmakers left these regions blank. Others filled them with guesses, decorative sea creatures, or imaginary islands that later explorers searched for in vain.

Historians sometimes describe the blank spaces as the most honest parts of an old map. A drawn coastline can be wrong in many ways, but an empty area admits plainly that the mapmaker does not know. In that sense, the emptiness carries information of its own. It tells the reader exactly where the trustworthy part of the map ends.

Imaginary islands proved remarkably stubborn. Once a mapmaker included one, other mapmakers often copied it, partly out of caution and partly out of habit. An island that no sailor had ever seen could survive on charts for generations, reprinted so many times that it gained the appearance of certainty. Removing it required someone willing to declare, in effect, that dozens of respected maps had been mistaken.

Modern satellite images have erased most blank spaces, at least on land. Yet scientists still produce maps with deliberate gaps, such as charts of the deep ocean floor, where large areas have been measured only roughly. These maps often mark the uncertain zones in a lighter shade. The practice reflects an old lesson: a map that confesses its limits is more useful than one that pretends to know everything.$q$, 'draft')
