-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ec8d744b-1d31-56d1-934a-eec6d0c0236b', 'rg11-figurative-language-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Inventory Day$q$, $q$Every January, Ortiz Secondhand closed for one day so the staff could count everything in the building. Luis had worked there since September, and he had heard the older employees talk about inventory day the way people talk about a blizzard: something to prepare for, survive, and describe afterward with pride.

At eight in the morning, his aunt Marisol, who owned the shop, handed him a clipboard. "You have the lamps," she said. "Every lamp, every shade, every bulb that has ever wandered in here."

The lamp corner was a forest. Tall brass floor lamps stood beside squat ceramic ones, and a tangle of cords covered the floor like roots. Luis started counting and immediately lost track. He started again, more slowly, writing each one down as he went.

By noon he had counted eighty-seven lamps and found a shade tucked inside another shade tucked inside a third. He held them up to his cousin Jun, who was counting picture frames across the aisle. Jun laughed. "The shop is playing games with you."

In the afternoon, Marisol walked through to check the numbers. Luis expected her to be pleased that his list was complete, and she was, but she was even happier about something else. Near the back, he had found a box of brand-new bulbs that nobody remembered buying.

"This is the strange thing about inventory," she said. "You spend all day counting what you already have, and somehow you end up with more than you started with."

Luis looked at the clipboard, now soft from being folded and unfolded all day. He realized she did not only mean the bulbs. He knew the shop now, every crowded corner of it.$q$, 'draft')
