-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('749dcbcc-aaba-5b63-9630-c346625f1c23', 'rg11-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading the Ice$q$, $q$Deep inside the ice sheets of Greenland and Antarctica lies one of the most detailed records of Earth's past climate. Each year, snow falls on the surface, and over time the weight of later snow presses it into solid ice. Tiny bubbles of air are sealed inside as this happens. When scientists drill out a long cylinder of ice, called an ice core, they are holding a frozen archive in their hands.

Researchers often say that an ice core lets them read the atmosphere of the distant past. By measuring the gases trapped in those bubbles, they can estimate how much carbon dioxide the air contained thousands of years ago. Layers of volcanic ash show when major eruptions occurred, and changes in the chemistry of the ice reveal whether a period was relatively warm or cold.

The work is painstaking. A core can stretch for more than a mile, and scientists slice it into sections, label each one, and ship them in freezers to laboratories far away. A single crack or a warm afternoon on a loading dock can destroy centuries of evidence.

There is something paradoxical about the whole enterprise. Ice is one of the most fragile materials scientists study, melting at a temperature people experience every winter, yet it has preserved information longer than almost any written document. Its permanence depends entirely on staying cold.

As glaciers shrink, some researchers worry that parts of this archive are disappearing before anyone can study them. In response, teams have begun storing extra cores in protected freezers, keeping a library of ice for scientists who have not yet been born.$q$, 'draft')
