-- Passage rg11-literary-structure-pov-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('95df3669-5f25-508b-a6d9-48db3e8976cb', 'rg11-literary-structure-pov-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Moving Day Inventory$q$, $q$Three tins of buttons, sorted by no system I can find.
One pincushion shaped like a tomato, leaking sawdust.
Forty spools of thread, most of them blue,
because, my grandmother says, blue was always the color she ran out of.

Patterns for a coat she never made.
A coat she made instead, from a pattern she never kept.
A yardstick with my height marked on it in pencil,
at ages four, six, nine, and twelve,
and then no more marks, because I stopped visiting on Sundays.

She tells me to take whatever I want.
She says the new apartment is very sensible.
It has a window that faces a parking lot,
and a closet with room for exactly one box.

I pack the buttons. I pack the tomato.
I wrap the yardstick in an old towel
and tell her it will fit in my car, which it will not.
She says that is fine, that is fine, that is very fine.

We carry the boxes down the narrow stairs
and stack them by the curb in the afternoon light.
The room upstairs is empty now, and very clean.
Somebody new will love it, she says.
I do not say that somebody already did.$q$, 'draft')
