-- Passage rg11-literary-structure-pov-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9617c6e6-cebe-592a-a2d3-06d729b873aa', 'rg11-literary-structure-pov-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Lane Four$q$, $q$We finished last, by eleven seconds, which in rowing is roughly the length of a lunch break. That is where this story ends, so I might as well start there.

In February, eight of us signed up for the new rowing club at Westbrook High mostly because the poster promised free sweatshirts. None of us had ever held an oar. Our boat was borrowed from a college across the river, and it had a long crack near the bow that our coach, Ms. Adeyemi, had patched with tape and optimism. Our coxswain, a small and fearless sophomore named Amara Lindqvist, had never steered anything larger than a shopping cart.

We practiced at dawn, when the water was flat and gray and the only other living things on the river were geese who seemed to find us embarrassing. For weeks, our oars entered the water at eight different moments. The boat rocked like a nervous animal. Ms. Adeyemi did not shout. She simply said, every morning, "Together. Not faster. Together."

At the regional regatta in May, we drew lane four. By the halfway mark, the other boats were well ahead, and we could hear their crowds cheering from the shore. Then, somewhere around five hundred meters from the finish, something changed. Eight blades caught the water at the same instant. The boat stopped rocking and began to run, light and quiet, as if the river had decided to carry us. Nobody spoke. Even Amara stopped calling the count, because she did not need to.

We still finished last, of course. Ms. Adeyemi met us at the dock. She did not mention the time. She asked whether we had felt it, at the five hundred mark, and all nine of us said yes at once, the same way we had finally learned to row.$q$, 'draft')
