  ('c7f9ad24-0373-5e3b-8248-c63055b7f41d', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'easy',
   $q$Why is the final sentence of the fable ironic?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It praises the heron sincerely for becoming the wisest bird on the river.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"takes-speaker-literally"},{"text":"It reveals that the heron secretly caught many fish before it left.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It calls knowledge a comfort when the heron's knowledge has left it hungry.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It shows that the other birds were jealous of the heron's ledger.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It calls knowledge a comfort when the heron's knowledge has left it hungry.$q$, $q$The narrator says knowledge is "a great comfort" on a flight "with an empty stomach," meaning the opposite: the knowledge did the heron no good. Read as sincere praise, the line ignores the hunger that ends the sentence.$q$, 'draft', '1f423d1b-86a2-5d31-bb69-326e28f42f48')
