  ('69fe5ca0-1ff6-59fa-bfbd-7e4e6ba957ef', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'medium',
   $q$How does the kingfisher function in the structure of the fable?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It acts as a narrator, showing the events from a second point of view above the river.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-narrator"},{"text":"It acts as a teacher, showing the heron step by step how to fish before the frost arrived.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It acts as a contrast, showing that simple action feeds a bird while endless study does not.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It acts as a villain, showing that the other birds tried to ruin the heron's careful plan.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-character"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It acts as a contrast, showing that simple action feeds a bird while endless study does not.$q$, $q$The kingfisher eats every day without any plan, which sets off the heron's growing ledger and shrinking body ("The ledger grew thick. The heron grew thin."). It never interferes with or instructs the heron.$q$, 'draft', '1f423d1b-86a2-5d31-bb69-326e28f42f48')
