-- Passage rg11-literary-structure-pov-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7ab98993-2aaa-5cad-9fd7-88d6ee505a25', 'rg11-literary-structure-pov-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Perfectly Calm$q$, $q$I was not nervous before my piano recital. I want that on the record, since my younger brother, Mateo, has been telling people otherwise.

It is true that I checked my sheet music eleven times in the car. That was not nerves. That was thoroughness, which is a quality admired by serious musicians. It is also true that my hands were shaking when we arrived at the auditorium, but that was because the building was cold, even though it was June and the air conditioning, as the janitor later mentioned, had been broken for a week.

Backstage, I practiced my bow in the bathroom mirror for twenty minutes. A calm person might wonder why anyone would practice bowing, and the answer is that bowing is part of the performance, and I am a professional. I explained this to Mateo three times. I also explained that nerves are for amateurs, and he asked why I kept explaining it, which I thought was a rude question for a nine-year-old.

When my name was called, I walked onto the stage at a reasonable speed, possibly slightly faster than reasonable. I played the first page of the sonata beautifully. On the second page, my left hand skipped a measure and came back in a beat late, which the audience surely interpreted as an artistic choice.

I finished, I bowed, and people clapped. My mother found me in the hallway afterward and handed me a bottle of water. I drank the entire thing without stopping, because, as I have already explained, the auditorium was very dry.$q$, 'draft')
