-- Passage rg11-literary-structure-pov-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e6cfc567-cd22-59c1-bc46-50a863751977', 'rg11-literary-structure-pov-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Friday Train$q$, $q$In the spring of 1891, the telegraph office in Cedar Bluff was run by Odalys Ferreira, who was quick, careful, and, on that particular Tuesday, recovering from a cold. The message for Mr. Okonkwo at the hardware store came through at noon: MEET BAND SAW FRIDAY TRAIN. Odalys sneezed halfway through copying it, and the word SAW never reached the paper.

Mr. Okonkwo was out of town until Friday, so the message was posted on the board outside the office, where messages for absent people were always posted. By supper, everyone in Cedar Bluff had read it. A band was coming. Nobody knew which band, and nobody minded.

The town took the news seriously. The schoolteacher, Miss Haldane, rehearsed her students in a welcome song. The Cedar Bluff Brass Society, which had six members and four working instruments, practiced a march every evening on the church steps. Mrs. Tanaka baked eleven pies, on the theory that musicians were always hungry. Someone hung a banner across Main Street that read WELCOME, FRIENDS OF MUSIC.

On Friday, half the county stood on the platform. The train arrived, the doors opened, and two porters carried out a single wooden crate stamped with the words BAND SAW, HANDLE WITH CARE. Mr. Okonkwo, stepping off behind it, looked at the crowd, the banner, and the trembling brass section, and understood everything at once.

There was a long silence. Then the Brass Society, having practiced all week, began to play. The children sang. Mrs. Tanaka's pies were served on the platform, and the crate was carried down Main Street at the head of a small parade. It was, the Cedar Bluff newspaper reported the next morning, the warmest welcome any piece of machinery had ever received in the county.$q$, 'draft')
