values
  ('c718bf8d-47a3-5e2c-a5cc-4a5c0bab19eb', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Which choice best explains why the story's resolution is comedic rather than tragic?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The town sends the crate back on the train, so Mr. Okonkwo loses his order but keeps his reputation.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The town learns the real band will arrive next week, so the welcome was only postponed and not wasted.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The town discovers who made the mistake and punishes the operator, so order is finally restored to Cedar Bluff.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The town turns a possible embarrassment into a shared celebration, so the community ends more united.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The town turns a possible embarrassment into a shared celebration, so the community ends more united.$q$, $q$After "a long silence," the band plays, the pies are served and the crate leads a parade, so the misunderstanding ends in festivity rather than loss or blame. Odalys is never blamed, and the crate stays in town.$q$, 'draft', 'e6cfc567-cd22-59c1-bc46-50a863751977')
