values
  ('e563e545-0101-5bf9-a7ea-fdcb5956b83b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'hard',
   $q$By ending with the town celebrating a crate, what does the author suggest about Cedar Bluff?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The town's telegraph office is unreliable, and the author wants it replaced by the newspaper.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"The town's hardware store is its most respected business, admired more than any band.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The town's eagerness to gather and celebrate matters more to it than the occasion itself.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The town's people are foolish and gullible, and the author wants readers to look down on them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The town's eagerness to gather and celebrate matters more to it than the occasion itself.$q$, $q$"Nobody knew which band, and nobody minded," and the crowd celebrates anyway once the truth is out, showing that the gathering is the real point. The humor is warm rather than scornful, so the author does not invite readers to look down on the town.$q$, 'draft', 'e6cfc567-cd22-59c1-bc46-50a863751977')
