-- Passage rg11-literary-structure-pov-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('53059e2f-498a-5425-8a0c-589494b02356', 'rg11-literary-structure-pov-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Mostly Air$q$, $q$The first thing Mr. Halvorsen taught me was that a violin is mostly air. I was fifteen, and I thought it was the kind of thing old craftsmen said to sound wise.

I had come to his workshop to learn to carve scrolls, the curled heads of violins that look like the inside of a seashell. Instead, he handed me a block of spruce and a small brass plane and told me to make the wood thinner. For four months I planed spruce. I planed it on Saturdays and after school, and I planed it while my friends were at the lake. Every week Mr. Halvorsen held my plate up to the window, tapped it with one knuckle, listened, and handed it back. "Thinner," he said, and nothing else.

I complained to my sister that he was using me as free labor. I complained to my mother that I could have learned more from a video. I did not complain to Mr. Halvorsen, because he would only have tapped something and said "thinner."

In the spring, he finally let me assemble a whole instrument. When I drew the bow across the strings for the first time, the sound did not seem to come from the wood at all. It came from the space inside, the dark, shaped emptiness that the thin plates enclosed and shook. The thick plates I had made in the autumn would have smothered it.

I worked in that shop for six more years. The last thing Mr. Halvorsen taught me, on the day he handed me his own planes and his key to the workshop, was that a violin is mostly air. It was the same sentence. I was the one who had changed.$q$, 'draft')
