-- Passage rg11-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a0f9180e-714c-52c6-9e4d-582f063048ad', 'rg11-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Town with Two Clocks$q$, $q$In the early nineteenth century, the fictional market town of Harrowgate kept time the way most towns of its era did: by the sun. Noon was the moment the sun stood highest over the town square, and the church clock and the shopkeepers' watches were set to match it. A town forty miles to the east kept its own noon a few minutes earlier, and nobody minded, because few people traveled between the two in a single day.

The arrival of the railway changed that. Trains ran on published timetables, and a timetable is only useful if everyone reading it means the same thing by eight o'clock. Each station along the line, however, kept its own local time. Passengers missed connections, and station workers had to keep careful records to keep trains on the single track safely apart.

The railway company's answer was to run every station on one standard time, taken from the clock at its main terminal in the capital. Harrowgate's station clock was moved forward eleven minutes. For several years the town lived with two times at once. The church clock still followed the sun, while the station clock followed the company, and some shops hung a second clock face so customers could check both.

The change was not purely practical. Some residents objected that the sun, not a distant company, ought to decide when noon arrived, and a few farmers ignored station time for decades. Gradually, though, newspapers, schools and the post office adopted it, because their business increasingly depended on the trains.

By the time Harrowgate's council made railway time official, the vote merely recognized what daily life had already decided. The story of the town's two clocks shows that a shared system of time was not a natural fact but an agreement, built slowly as new technology tied distant places together.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
