   $q$Which is the best objective summary of the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The writer argues that Westmere should require downward, dimmer and timed outdoor lights. The writer says this would cut wasted energy and harm to wildlife, and claims shielded lights are as safe as glaring ones.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The writer argues that Westmere should remove all outdoor lighting because studies prove that lighting does not improve safety and only harms insects and birds.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"distorts-claim"},{"text":"Older people in Westmere remember a sky full of stars, but children today can see only a few dozen. The writer misses the way the town used to look at night.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Westmere has wasted a third of its lighting budget for years and ruined the night sky. The town must act now, and anyone who opposes the standard simply does not care about wildlife or the stars.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"includes-opinion"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The writer argues that Westmere should require downward, dimmer and timed outdoor lights. The writer says this would cut wasted energy and harm to wildlife, and claims shielded lights are as safe as glaring ones.$q$, $q$An objective summary of an argument reports what the writer claims without taking sides, as the choice beginning "The writer argues that Westmere should require downward" does. The choice saying anyone who opposes the standard "simply does not care" adopts the writer's position and adds its own attack on opponents.$q$, 'draft', '0f82031e-88f1-5a7f-a969-d3e6f4fc3e6a')
