-- Passage rg11-main-idea-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fe8e373c-6b7b-5d50-b66f-a797b8a135e6', 'rg11-main-idea-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Eight Rowers, One Rhythm$q$, $q$To a spectator on the riverbank, a rowing crew looks like eight people pulling as hard as they can. Strength matters, but coaches will tell you that the fastest boats are not always the strongest. They are the boats in which every rower moves in the same rhythm, so that the boat runs smoothly between strokes instead of lurching.

The reason lies in how a racing shell moves. The boat is long, narrow and light, and it slows down every time the rowers slide forward to take the next stroke. If one rower's blade enters the water a fraction of a second early, it acts briefly as a brake. If another pulls out late, the boat tips to one side and the others must correct it, wasting effort. A crew of powerful but unmatched rowers can lose to a weaker crew that moves as one.

For this reason, training focuses heavily on timing. Crews spend long sessions rowing at low pressure, concentrating on matching the moment each blade catches the water. Many boats carry a small device that records the boat's speed many times per second, and coaches study the graphs for dips that reveal when a rower is out of time. The person sitting nearest the stern, called the stroke, sets the rhythm, and everyone else follows that rower's movements.

At the fictional Linwood Rowing Club, coaches tested this idea over one season. Instead of selecting their top eight athletes by strength scores alone, they rotated rowers through lineups and kept the combination whose speed graph was smoothest. That crew included two rowers with below-average strength scores, yet it finished the season with the club's fastest times.

The lesson reaches beyond rowing. In many group efforts, from orchestras to relay teams, the combined result depends not only on what each member can do alone but on how well their efforts fit together.$q$, 'draft')
