   $q$Which is the best objective summary of the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Strong rowers often tip the boat, so coaches at Linwood removed their strongest athletes. The crew with no strong rowers then won every race of the season.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"distorts-detail"},{"text":"Rowing coaches have wrongly focused on strength for too long. Every coach should follow Linwood's excellent example and stop choosing athletes by how strong they are.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"includes-opinion"},{"text":"Many racing boats carry a device that records speed many times per second. Coaches look at the graphs for dips, and the stroke sets the rhythm for everyone else.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"A racing shell loses speed when rowers are out of time, so crews train hard to match their strokes. At one club, the smoothest lineup was fastest despite weaker members, a lesson that applies to other group efforts.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A racing shell loses speed when rowers are out of time, so crews train hard to match their strokes. At one club, the smoothest lineup was fastest despite weaker members, a lesson that applies to other group efforts.$q$, $q$The choice beginning "A racing shell loses speed" covers the cause, the training, the Linwood test and the broader lesson without judgment. The choice saying the crew "won every race" distorts paragraph 4, which says only two rowers had below-average scores and the crew had the club's fastest times, not that it won every race.$q$, 'draft', 'fe8e373c-6b7b-5d50-b66f-a797b8a135e6')
