insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('abc639b4-7adb-5242-af70-ee4acc8917c8', 'rg11-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Voices of Brackenridge$q$, $q$When the fictional mill town of Brackenridge marked its two hundredth anniversary, the public library's local history room held hundreds of documents: deeds, council minutes, newspaper clippings and the payroll books of the old textile mill. What it lacked were the voices of the people who had actually worked the looms, run the corner shops and raised families in the brick row houses along the river.

A group of high school students set out to fill that gap. With help from a librarian, they spent a school year recording interviews with forty older residents. Before each interview, the students studied the topic, prepared open questions, and learned to let silences stretch so that speakers had time to remember. Each recording was then transcribed, checked with the speaker, and stored in the library with a short summary.

The interviews added details that no official record contained. The mill's payroll books listed wages and hours, but former workers described the roar of the machines, the system of hand signals they used because no one could hear over the noise, and the lunch hours spent on the riverbank. One woman explained how families pooled money to help a neighbor whose house had flooded, an event the newspaper had covered in a single sentence.

Oral histories have limits, and the students learned to treat them carefully. Memories fade and sometimes shift over decades, and two residents might describe the same event in different ways. The students compared interviews with each other and with written records, noting where accounts agreed and where they differed rather than choosing one version as the truth.

The collection is now among the most requested materials in the library. Teachers use it in local history units, and families visit to hear a grandparent's voice. The project showed that a town's history lives not only in its files but in its people, and that gathering those voices responsibly takes patience, preparation and a habit of checking.$q$, 'draft')
