-- Passage rg11-story-details-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5328590d-02a6-5026-a1df-ae20433c00ad', 'rg11-story-details-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Auditorium Piano$q$, $q$Mr. Osei arrived at the Hollis High auditorium at seven, an hour before the first bell, carrying a canvas bag that clinked when he set it down. Lucia Ferreira, who ran the stage crew and therefore had a key, had been told to let him in and stay out of his way. She managed the first part.

The piano sat under a gray cover at stage left, where it had sat for as long as anyone could remember. A flyer taped to the green room door announced that the music department was raising money for a new instrument, and someone had drawn a small frowning face on the picture of the old piano in the flyer's corner.

Mr. Osei folded back the cover, lifted the lid, and played a single low note. He listened to it fade completely before he played the next one. Lucia had expected a song, or at least a scale, but for twenty minutes he played one note at a time, turning a pin with a short metal lever and listening again.

"Is it bad?" she finally asked.

"It is sixty years old," he said, which was not an answer.

When he was finished, he reached under the lid and drew out a thin notebook she had never known was there. Its cover was as soft as cloth. Inside, in a dozen different hands, were dates and short notes going back to 1971: Tuned, humid spring. Replaced two hammers. Bass strings original, still fine. One entry, from 1988, said only: Played by the whole senior class at graduation. Held.

Mr. Osei wrote the date and three lines in small, upright letters. He did not offer to let her read them, but he also did not close the notebook right away. Instead he set it on the music stand, open and facing outward, while he packed his bag and walked up the aisle without looking back.

After the door swung shut, Lucia stood at the stand for a long moment. Then she took a photograph of the open page, put the notebook back exactly where it had been, and went to find the music teacher before homeroom.$q$, 'draft')
