-- Passage rg11-story-details-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2cf481d5-3cf4-5436-a134-7e4cbc0c6080', 'rg11-story-details-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Doorframe, Apartment 4C$q$, $q$The movers wrapped the lamps in blankets
as if the lamps had ever been afraid of anything.
By noon the rooms were only rooms again,
square and loud, the light too honest on the floor.

My mother counted boxes in the hallway.
My brother counted nothing. He sat on the stairs
and tied his shoes, untied them, tied them again,
a job he could finish.

In the kitchen doorway, low on the left side,
were the pencil lines: my name, his name, dates
in my father's careful print, climbing the paint
like a ladder somebody forgot to take down.

The landlord had already left a can of white
by the sink, its lid tapped shut, a brush across the top.
He was a kind man. He had waited a week.
He would paint on Monday.

I did not take a picture. I thought of it.
Instead I took the pencil from the junk drawer,
the last thing left in it, and stood my brother
against the frame and made him stand up straight.

He was taller than the top line by a hand.
I wrote the date. I did not write his name.
I wanted whoever came next to wonder
who had grown here, and how much, and why
they stopped measuring, and why they started again.$q$, 'draft')
