-- Passage rg11-story-details-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('79af5ab3-d9e4-57f8-b7c8-5744625a1d25', 'rg11-story-details-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Clock on Board Four$q$, $q$Mei-Lin Zhao needed one more win. Her coach had said so twice in the hallway, once with numbers and once without, and both versions meant the same thing: win the last round of the Carroway Open and she would qualify for the state championship in March. Draw or lose, and she would be watching it online.

Her final opponent was a boy named Dario Novak, who looked about twelve and had brought his own cushion to sit on. He set up the pieces with great care, turning each knight so that it faced forward, and then he set the clock.

Mei-Lin glanced at it and stopped. Her side read sixty minutes. His side read forty-five.

It was an easy mistake to make. The buttons on that model were tiny, and the arbiters had warned everyone twice that morning to check. Dario had not checked. He was already writing her name at the top of his scoresheet, spelling it carefully.

Fifteen minutes is a great deal of time in a close game. Mei-Lin looked at the clock for what felt like a long while. Then she raised her hand.

The arbiter fixed it in under a minute. Dario turned red to the ears and said thank you without looking up.

The game took almost two hours. Somewhere in the middle Mei-Lin made a careless move with her bishop, and Dario, who played like someone much older, found the reply she had missed. By the end he had a pawn that could not be stopped, and both of them knew it. She tipped her king over and offered her hand.

In the hallway her coach was waiting, already reading the result on his phone. He opened his mouth, and before he could begin, Mei-Lin said, "His clock was wrong at the start. I told the arbiter."

Her coach closed his mouth. For a moment he looked at her as if she had made a move he had not expected. Then he took her scoresheet, folded it once, and put it in his jacket pocket, which was where he kept the ones he planned to study.$q$, 'draft')
