-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f6d1ed59-e470-53ef-8d27-6c1dd45de249', 'rg11-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Let Seniors Show Their Work$q$, $q$Every June at Westbrook High School, seniors spend a week filling in bubbles on final exams, and then the results vanish into a gradebook that no one outside the school ever sees. I propose a different ending to high school: each senior should replace one final exam with a public portfolio exhibition, in which students present a substantial piece of work to a panel of teachers, family members and community volunteers.

The first reason is practical. Exams measure what a student can recall in ninety minutes, but most work after high school looks more like a project, with research, drafts, revision and a final product. An exhibition asks students to practice exactly that cycle.

The second reason concerns motivation. When the only audience for a lab report is one teacher with a red pen, it is easy to do the minimum. When a student knows that a retired engineer from the neighborhood will ask questions about her bridge design, the work suddenly matters to someone real.

The third reason, and the most important, is that exhibitions reveal thinking that tests hide. Last spring, a pilot group of fifteen Westbrook seniors presented exhibitions instead of taking their history final. Panelists reported that students who had earned middling exam grades all year often gave the most thoughtful answers when asked how they had changed their minds during their research. A multiple-choice test had no way to capture that.

Some teachers worry that exhibitions take too long to grade and are harder to score fairly than an exam with an answer key. That concern deserves a serious answer. The pilot used a shared scoring guide with four clear categories, and pairs of panelists agreed on scores in most cases. Grading did take longer, but teachers described those hours as the most rewarding of their year.

High school should end with students showing what they can do, not just what they can remember. One exhibition per senior is a modest change with a large payoff.$q$, 'draft')
