-- Passage rg11-theme-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('84cd6bc4-cabe-557e-b99a-2929b048090e', 'rg11-theme-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Last Mile at Harlow Ridge$q$, $q$Priya Raman had circled one number on the calendar above her desk: 18:40, the time she needed to qualify for the state meet. For three seasons she had chased it the way she chased everything, alone, with a watch that beeped every quarter mile and a training log that recorded each split in neat columns.

At Harlow Ridge, the course climbed for the first two miles and then dropped through pine woods toward the finish. Priya reached the top of the last hill four seconds ahead of her plan. The watch on her wrist told her what she already felt: this was the day.

Then she saw Simone Ferreira at the edge of the trail, bent over with one hand pressed to her side. Simone had beaten Priya in every race since ninth grade. They were not friends, exactly. They nodded at each other at starting lines and studied each other's results online.

"Keep going," Simone gasped. "I'm fine."

She was not fine. Her stride had collapsed into a stagger, and the next runners were still far behind. Priya looked at her watch. Then she slowed, fell in beside Simone, and matched her breathing to the other girl's ragged rhythm.

"Out for three, in for two," Priya said. "Your coach yells that at you. I've heard him."

Simone almost laughed. They ran the last mile together, not fast, through the needles and the cool shade. Somewhere in that mile the cramp loosened, and by the finish chute Simone was pulling slightly ahead. Priya let her.

The clock read 19:12. Priya's watch confirmed it with a cheerful beep, as if nothing had been lost.

Afterward Simone found her by the water table and held out half of an orange. Neither of them said much. On the bus home, Priya opened her training log and stared at the empty space beside the date. Finally she wrote the time, and under it, in smaller letters, a sentence that was not a split: Ran the last mile with someone.$q$, 'draft')
