-- Passage rg11-theme-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bab7329c-41a8-5098-83e6-e160f484dcd1', 'rg11-theme-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Cartographer and the River$q$, $q$In a valley where the river wandered, there lived a cartographer named Orrin who was famous for the accuracy of his maps. Merchants paid twice his rivals' price, because travelers who carried an Orrin map never lost their way.

One spring the river flooded, and when the water fell it had cut a new channel a quarter mile to the east. The old bed dried into a long ribbon of reeds. Travelers began returning to Orrin's shop with mud on their boots and complaints in their mouths. The ford marked on his map led only to a marsh.

"The river is in the wrong place," Orrin told them. "My map was drawn with great care. Wait a season, and it will return."

A young surveyor named Liesl, who had once been his apprentice, set up a table across the square. She sold rough maps sketched in a single afternoon, with the new channel penciled in and a note at the bottom: Checked this week. Her lines were less elegant than his. Her prices were lower, and her maps were right.

By midsummer Orrin's shop was quiet. He spent the evenings polishing his old copper plates and telling the few visitors who came that the valley would come to its senses.

One evening a child came in looking for a map to her grandmother's farm. Orrin unrolled his finest sheet and pointed. The child frowned. "But the bridge you drew washed away," she said. "I watched it go."

Orrin looked at the map for a long while. Then, for the first time in thirty years, he picked up a pencil instead of an engraving tool, walked down to the new riverbank, and began to measure.

The next spring his maps carried a small line at the bottom, in plain lettering beneath the fine engraving. It said: Checked this week.$q$, 'draft')
