-- Passage rg11-word-parts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a2ed043a-a576-5361-a12b-59833aa13adb', 'rg11-word-parts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Closing Argument$q$, $q$Priya Raman had always been the loquacious member of her debate pair. She could talk for ten minutes about almost anything, and her coach joked that she needed a stopwatch more than a notebook. Her partner, Tobias Okafor, rarely spoke in class, so when he volunteered to deliver the closing argument at the regional tournament, Priya was surprised.

The resolution was ordinary: the school library should stay open two hours later on weeknights. Priya and Tobias were assigned to advocate for the change, and they had gathered evidence about students who lacked quiet places to study at home.

During practice, Tobias wrote his speech in a casual, colloquial style, full of phrases like "kind of" and "you know." Their coach, Ms. Delacroix, read it and frowned. "Judges want clear language," she said. "Talk to them the way you'd write a strong essay, not the way you'd chat in the hallway."

That night Tobias rewrote every paragraph. He paced his bedroom and rehearsed aloud, delivering what his younger sister called a soliloquy to the bookshelf. By morning his sentences were precise and his examples were vivid.

At the tournament, the opposing team was vocal from the start, interrupting with rapid questions. Priya answered most of them, but she left the final minutes to Tobias. He stood, took a breath, and spoke slowly about a classmate who did homework on the bus because the library closed at four.

The room went silent. Afterward, a judge wrote on the ballot that the closing was the most eloquent speech of the round. Priya read the comment twice and grinned. "Next time," she said, "you're doing the talking."$q$, 'draft')
