values
  ('21078fa6-3f78-5bc1-af82-ab5955a34cf7', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Priya is described as "loquacious," and the judge calls Tobias's speech "eloquent." Both words contain loqu, and the prefix e- means "out." Which statement best explains the difference between them?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Loquacious is a compliment, while eloquent suggests someone talks too much.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-connotation"},{"text":"Loquacious stresses how much someone talks; eloquent stresses how well and persuasively.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Loquacious describes written work; eloquent describes only spoken work.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"},{"text":"Both words mean the same thing, so the judge was really praising Priya.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-affix-change"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Loquacious stresses how much someone talks; eloquent stresses how well and persuasively.$q$, $q$The different affixes change the meaning: loquacious points to quantity of talk, while eloquent means speaking out clearly and movingly, which fits the silent room after Tobias's slow, vivid closing. The words are not synonyms, since the judge praised the quiet partner, not the talkative one.$q$, 'draft', 'a2ed043a-a576-5361-a12b-59833aa13adb')
