-- Passage rg11-word-parts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('995df5ae-8188-5b3e-9d61-2a6ea1f2e03c', 'rg11-word-parts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Detectives of the Forest$q$, $q$When a tree begins to wilt or its leaves spot with brown patches, the people called to investigate are often plant pathologists. These scientists spend their careers identifying what makes plants sick and how the damage spreads from one tree to the next.

The culprit is frequently a pathogen, a microscopic organism such as a fungus, bacterium, or virus. Not every problem has that kind of cause, however. A tree planted in poor soil may simply be malnourished, lacking the nutrients it needs, and its yellowing leaves can look surprisingly similar to an infection. Distinguishing between the two is one of the first tasks in any investigation.

Some outbreaks have reshaped entire landscapes. In the early twentieth century, a fungal blight swept through forests in eastern North America and killed billions of chestnut trees within a few decades. The fungus showed remarkable virulence, spreading quickly and overwhelming trees that had no natural defense against it.

Researchers have spent generations searching for solutions. Some have crossbred surviving trees with related species that resist the blight. Others have studied weaker strains of the fungus that seem to slow the disease. Progress has been gradual, but the persistence of these scientists has produced young chestnuts that may one day restore part of the old forest.

Public attitudes matter too. When people respond to plant diseases with apathy, assuming the problem belongs to someone else, small outbreaks can grow unnoticed for years. Volunteers who learn to recognize early symptoms and report them give researchers a valuable head start.$q$, 'draft')
